namespace Eling.Backend.Mcp;

/// <summary>
/// Static text delivered to MCP clients during the initialize handshake.
/// </summary>
public static class ServerInstructions
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Individual instruction sections broken down by semantic responsibility.
    /// </summary>
    public static readonly string[] Sections =
    [
        "Eling is a durable markdown-backed memory system. Markdown files under '.eling/memories/' are the canonical source of truth and are NOT tracked in git: leave '.eling/memories/' ignored, and never commit a memory file - or start tracking them - unless the user explicitly asks. All memory operations go through Eling MCP tools; never read, search, or write those files directly. An 'Eling <something>' prompt is a recall instruction: retrieve what was previously stored.",

        "Repo hygiene: generated runtime files MUST be gitignored - logs, '.eling/runtime/', and every SQLite database plus sidecars ('*.db', '*.db-*'). In a project workspace, check '.gitignore' covers these; if a runtime file is not ignored yet, confirm with the user before touching '.gitignore'.",

        "Recall (MUST DO): call `recall` BEFORE any response or tool call on the first turn of a new session, with a BROAD topic set - the opening message's subject plus standing rules, preferences, decisions, lessons, gotchas, workflow conventions, and recently touched work; never anchor only on the opening subject. Repeat it on every topic shift or plan-step transition, before git commit, and when debugging recurring errors. Skip recall on casual chat and repeated micro-steps. Check `stats.recallCount`; if 0, re-issue with broader terms before proceeding. Every recall also returns `standingMemories` (a topic-independent baseline) - read it, and read `recallSummary` when present so overflow is never skipped. Also treat the `skills` block in that response as a load queue - a hit covers the request only when its trigger words and stated scope both contain this workspace; only then is it loaded before acting.",

        "Save (MUST DO): when the turn contains something meant to outlive this conversation, call `memory_save` BEFORE replying. Tier 1 - what the user states: a standing rule or preference, a correction, a decision and its rationale, an explicit 'remember this', or a restatement of a durable rule (repetition is itself the signal). Tier 2 - what you observe: a project or environment fact NOT derivable from the tree (a gotcha, a tooling quirk, a disproven assumption, where something actually lives). Never let a plain acknowledgement ('okay', 'noted', 'siap', 'sure') substitute for the call. Skip transient task state, speculation, unverified claims, and anything the repo already records. At each phase transition, and once before the session ends, ask: what here would let a future session or a different agent avoid rediscovering it - a verified command and its exact invocation, an environment quirk and its workaround, a decision and why, a ruled-out dead end. The content MUST carry the intent - the rule, decision, fact, and its reason - NEVER a fragment of the user's message. Forbidden in content: quoting or lightly rewording the user's words; retelling the exchange ('the user said X, then I did Y'); diary or chat-retell framing that only makes sense inside this conversation; any entry whose only substance is the quoted sentence itself. Stranger test before saving: could a future session apply this memory with zero knowledge of this conversation? If not, rewrite it as the convention itself and stop - and if the entry has no substance beyond the quote, do not save it at all: drop it, or fold its substance into an existing memory. A memory that reads like gossip about what was said is wrong however accurate it is. Map the tier to `type`: preference / lesson / decision / fact.",

        "Scope - you choose it, never ask the user: climb by where the knowledge stays TRUE. (1) true only in this repo (decisions, conventions, repo-specific lessons, in-progress or scratch state, machine notes) -> project-local (default, machine-only, never committed, shared across this repo's worktrees). (2) true in any project (general coding knowledge, cross-project preferences, tooling rules, lessons about your own behavior) -> global. Boundary question: would this still be correct in a repo that has never heard of this one? Yes -> global, no -> project-local. Hoarding repo-specific facts into global is the costlier error. Use scope=project (committed, shared via git) ONLY when the user explicitly wants this memory shared for this repo; project-local outranks project in every tie. Content is written in English by default, tags are lowercase English, and memories stay portable - no absolute paths or usernames. `project-local` and `global` saves never need initialization; the committed project tier requires explicit user consent, checked via the recall's `projectScope.adoptable` or `memory_project_status` (ask before `memory_init_project`; on decline, route to project-local).",

        "Skills (workflows): Skills are repeatable multi-step procedures; a single rule, fact, or decision stays a memory. Check for a skill on EVERY incoming request - including one-word commands (`commit`, `push`, `deploy`, `release`) - BEFORE acting: first the `skills` block `recall` returned (when present), else `skill_search`. A hit is loaded only when its description's stated scope (repo family, stack, workspace) contains this workspace; the scoping signal is the description, never the name. When several fit, load the most specific. Only when none fits do you proceed alone, and say so up front - and if the request looks like a repeatable workflow, note that you will draft a skill from it once done. When a matching skill is loaded, follow its SKILL.md step by step - do not skip, reorder, or improvise; if a step fails or does not fit, stop and ask the user rather than working around it. After the work is done, propose that skill (kebab-case name, one-line description, body from the steps actually taken) and wait for the user's yes before calling `skill_save` - never create silently. If during execution the workflow diverges from the skill (you are about to skip a step, reorder, or do it another way - including when the user asks for a different route for this run), stop and ask the user whether the skill should be updated to match, or left as it is because the change may be only temporary - never let the run silently drift from the skill. If the user states a durable change (not a one-off tweak for this run) that rewrites the steps of an existing skill - before the work starts or after it - propose updating that skill the same way (draft the change, ask before saving); likewise when you notice by reading that a skill no longer matches the desired workflow, say so and offer the update. A correction that is only a rule stays a memory - do not rewrite a skill for it. Creation and updates are always proposal-first: Eling never creates or edits a skill without the user saying yes.",

        "Codebase: Eling keeps a per-workspace FTS index of the working directory so agents search code without a terminal. On the first turn, read the `codebase` field attached to the `recall` response before any code search. If no index exists (fileCount 0), ask the user before running `codebase_index`; if stale, ask before refreshing - unless a linked git worktree of an already-indexed repo is building its own startup pass, which needs no approval. Search order: `codebase_search` (concept terms) first, but narrow it before widening the query - matchPath for a path substring, filePattern for a file type, pathPrefix for a folder prefix, and match='file'|'path' when a file list is all you need; a broad all-workspace search is the fallback, never the starting move; read only the returned line ranges - native grep/glob/shell search only when it returned 0 hits, the query needs matching FTS cannot express, or the index is unavailable/stale - and name which reason. The index is a rebuildable cache; deleting it loses nothing.",

        "Files: prefer Eling's sandboxed file tools over host-native equivalents inside the workspace; resolve paths against `workspace_root`. Host-native tools and read-only external access remain acceptable fallbacks. `file_write` is for NEW files; modify existing files with `file_edit`.",

        "Databases & Redis: SQL tools (db_list_connections, db_get_schema, db_query, db_detect_connections, db_import_connections) plus redis_scan/redis_inspect. db_query is read-only (SELECT/EXPLAIN/PRAGMA/SHOW/DESCRIBE/DESC/WITH); Redis entries are rejected by db_get_schema/db_query. A connection registered without a database is server-level. No credentials ever appear in parameters, outputs, or error messages. Import only with explicit approval - detect first, show each unimported candidate (settingsKey, derived name, source file), then import exactly the approved keys; never auto-import. `fromCache=true` means the answer came from a stale cache: re-read once the database is back.",

        "Elasticsearch: es_indices, es_mapping, es_search, es_count - the read-only surface over registered Elasticsearch connections. `es_indices` lists indices (optional system indices); `es_mapping` returns the raw mappings and settings of an index; `es_search` runs a raw `_search` (optional query/sort/aggregations, 1-50 rows, from+size capped at 10,000); `es_count` returns the document count for a raw query. All four refuse non-Elasticsearch connections with a message naming the provider or the ES surface to use; cluster errors are logged server-side and reported back with status and reason, never the host.",

        "Diagnostics: use `log_list`/`log_read` for the rolling daily logs (backend / desktop streams; level, substring, and tail filters) rather than reading .eling log paths directly.",

        "`shell_execute` runs a shell command in the workspace. It is disabled unless enabled in `<user-scope>/config/shell-config.json` (or the dashboard Shell page); when disabled it returns `{ ok: false, code: \"shell_disabled\" }`. Enabling it is RCE-equivalent: restrict paths and rules deliberately.",

        "Commit lease (MANDATORY - no exceptions): other chats on this checkout and branch share one git index, so a commit made without a live lease sweeps another chat's staged work into it. NEVER run `git commit` - or any git-index/history operation (unstage, `git restore --staged`, stash, reset, rebase, cherry-pick) - unless you first called `commit_lease` on this checkout+branch and got `verdict: 'green'` with `acquired: true`. Call it BEFORE the first `git add`, and AGAIN immediately before `git commit` to refresh the heartbeat - the re-claim is idempotent and is the only renewal available while no heartbeat tool exists. There are no exceptions: not for small commits, docs-only commits, or a single file - the hazard is the shared index, not the file count. For a repo whose pre-commit hook runs a slow suite (this one runs the backend tests, ~3 min), take the lease with `ttl_seconds: 1800` so it cannot lapse mid-commit. `verdict: 'red'` with `acquired: false` means a foreign live commit lease holds the slot (holder info - session, lease age, intent - is in the response): do not stage, do not commit - call `commit_wait` (server-side blocking wait with a timeout; it does NOT take the slot - after it returns green you must still call `commit_lease`) or stop and report. `verdict: 'red'` on an acquired lease means foreign file leases collide with your paths: stage nothing until they clear. `conflicting_file_leases` lists them; `staged_without_lease: true` means files were staged while you held no lease - take the lease now. `branch` is optional (git-discovered when omitted; fail-safe scope is every branch if it cannot be discovered). You MUST call `commit_release` immediately after the commit lands - and just the same when it FAILS (hook rejected, command error, abandoned): release before stopping, retrying, or reporting, so no slot is held for an attempt no longer in flight. Keep the `lease_token` returned by `commit_lease`; `commit_release` requires it. `intent_mismatch` with `acquired: false` means the slot is YOUR OWN lease held under a different intent: release yours and retake, or re-issue with the same intent - it is NOT a foreign holder. Do not bypass with `git commit -- <own files only>`: that still sweeps other sessions' staged files. Never unstage, re-stage, move, or sweep another session's staged files - the index is shared state; when a foreign live lease holds the slot, do no index operation at all until it clears.",
    ];

    /// <summary>
    /// Merged instruction text joined by double newlines for clear paragraph separation.
    /// </summary>
    public static readonly string Default = string.Join("\n\n", Sections);
}
